WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
THE STATE OF CARVANA:
Over the past 12 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:08.480
Carvana stock is up 750% meaning that if you&nbsp;
invested $1,000 at the beginning of 2023, you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:15.600
now have $8,500. Hearing this, you’d probably&nbsp;
be inclined to think that Carvana is killing it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.240
but if we zoom out a bit more, you’ll see a&nbsp;
completely different picture. Zooming out to 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.600
years, you’ll see that Carvana stock has actually&nbsp;
fallen off a cliff. From their peak in late 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:36.840
Carvana stock literally fell 99% by the end&nbsp;
of 2022 from $380 to $3.63. And even with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:42.000
their massive rally over the past 12 months,&nbsp;
Carvana stock is still down nearly 90% from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.880
the peak. Given all the market volatility,&nbsp;
high interest rates, and high inflation, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.440
would be easy to just write off this decline as a&nbsp;
stock that was hit hard by the macro environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.760
but there’s a lot more to this story. From&nbsp;
registration and title issues to stolen cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.040
and thousands of complaints. During the worst&nbsp;
of it, Carvana’s annual losses ballooned to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:10.640
much as $1.58 billion. As such, Caravana’s decline&nbsp;
very much has to do with poor fundamentals as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.920
as it does with a poor macroeconomic environment.&nbsp;
But, it wasn’t always like this. Not too long ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.560
Carvana was the startup super star that had&nbsp;
revolutionized the used car market. They had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:26.920
made it easier than ever to sell and buy cars&nbsp;
all from the comfort of your phone without having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.920
to deal with a single car salesman. That’s&nbsp;
why at their peak, Carvana was worth nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:39.520
$70 billion. For perspective, that’s nearly as&nbsp;
much as Mercedes Benz, and the only automotive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.960
companies that really outshine that number&nbsp;
is Toyota and Tesla. The good news, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.800
is that not all is lost for Carvana. In fact,&nbsp;
Carvana has been staging one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:58.040
comebacks we’ve ever seen. They just posted their&nbsp;
first real quarterly profit of $782 million and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:03.520
it seems that the nearly 10X stock recovery is&nbsp;
just the beginning. But, what about all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:09.880
serious concerns regarding stolen cars and invalid&nbsp;
titles? Has that been resolved? Well, let’s find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:27.058
out by taking a closer look the contoversial&nbsp;
rise, fall, and rise again of Carvana.

00:02:27.058 --> 00:02:29.400
CONTROVERSIAL ORIGINS:
If you think that Carvana has had a controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.240
past, just wait till you hear about the story of&nbsp;
the founders because it’s far more controversial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:41.360
Taking a look back, the story of Carvana takes us&nbsp;
back to a convicted felon named Ernest Garcia II.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.720
Garcia the II came from a pretty well off family&nbsp;
in New Mexico. His father not only owned a liquor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:52.440
store but he was the mayor of Gallup, New Mexico,&nbsp;
so safe to say that Gracia the II grew up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.840
some pretty powerful friends. And in classic&nbsp;
villain fashion, Garcia the II wouldn’t waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.920
anytime in leveraging these connections to enrich&nbsp;
himself which brings us into his first business:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:08.360
real estate development. There’s obviously a&nbsp;
lot of money to be made with real estate but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:14.720
Garcia the II would often find himself entangled&nbsp;
with shady deals. In October of 1990, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.040
Garcia the II would plead guilty to fraudulently&nbsp;
obtaining a $30 million line of credit while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.120
simultaneously helping a local bank hide their&nbsp;
land ownership from regulators. This would earn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.800
him 3 years of probation and his firm filing for&nbsp;
bankruptcy. Having burnt his reputation within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:36.760
real estate world, Garcia would make the jump&nbsp;
to an adjacent industry: car sales. In 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:42.800
he would purchase a bankrupt rent a car franchise&nbsp;
called Ugly Duckling for a $1 million. He didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.600
exactly know how to turn around the renting model,&nbsp;
so he would just turn the company into a used car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.840
dealership that targetted subprime car buyers. To&nbsp;
be honest, Garcia wasn’t really in the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:59.160
of selling cars but rather handing out loans to&nbsp;
delinquent borrowers. In fact, as much as 45% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.080
their car loans are delinquent at any given time,&nbsp;
and they actually have 370 full time employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.040
to collect these overdue payments. Selling cars&nbsp;
to naive people that they can’t afford and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.000
ruthlessly collecting turned out to be a pretty&nbsp;
profitable business model. By the turn of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.360
century, the Ugly Duckling was a public company&nbsp;
with annual revenues of $600 million. At this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.800
point, Garcia would decide to rebrand the company&nbsp;
in order move further away from his checkered past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.280
leading to the new name: DriveTime Automotive,&nbsp;
you might’ve heard of it. DriveTime would go onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.760
earn billions of dollars every single year which&nbsp;
brings us into the next generation of Garcias:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:42.000
Ernest Garcia III. Garcia the III would follow the&nbsp;
classic path of a rich kids son. He would attend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.440
a prestigious university which in his case was&nbsp;
Stanford before coming back to join the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:52.680
business in 2007. Oftentimes, these kids are the&nbsp;
ones the run the family business into the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.720
but that wasn’t the case with Garcia the III. He&nbsp;
was determined to take the family business to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.440
wholenother stratosphere. He had grown up seeing&nbsp;
the rise of the internet and dotcom companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.680
like Amazon and eBay. He knew that people weren’t&nbsp;
quite ready to make massive purchases like cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:13.600
fully online just yet, but he speculated that&nbsp;
it was just a matter of time. And if anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.360
had the resources, influence, and power to pull&nbsp;
this off, it was him. So, in 2012, with the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:29.480
backing of Drivetime, it’s executive team, and&nbsp;
his father, Garcia the III would launch Carvana.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:36.320
CONTROVERSIAL SUCCESS:
With all of that context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:42.600
it’s really no surprise why Carvana went on to&nbsp;
be a massive success. Carvana was no ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:48.000
startup created by a couple of college students.&nbsp;
Carvana was a strategic and calculated extension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:54.600
to an already massive family fortune. Drivetime,&nbsp;
or basically Garcia the II, provided Carvana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:00.360
or basically his son, with the cars he needed, the&nbsp;
financing he needed, the technology he needed, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.960
the expertise he needed. Technically, Garcia the&nbsp;
II isn’t one of Carvana’s directors or officers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.560
but don’t let that fool you. This was a strategic&nbsp;
move to keep Carvana separate from Garcia the II’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.560
past. And behind the scenes, the Garcia’s are not&nbsp;
only close but they literally live next to door to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.880
each other. Not to mention, Garcia the II is still&nbsp;
the company’s largest shareholder with a stake of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:30.120
roughly 25%. Also, the father son duo owns super&nbsp;
voting shares in Carvana which gives them full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:36.040
control of the company. But, I think that’s enough&nbsp;
about the Garcia family because there’s plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:41.560
controversy to cover regarding Carvana itself.&nbsp;
It’s not that Carvana is a direct scam. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:46.720
order a car, you will indeed get your car, and if&nbsp;
you a sell a car, you will indeed get your money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:52.160
But, there’s a lot more to buying and selling cars&nbsp;
than just transacting cash and authorizing loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.960
and this is where Carvana really falls short. Over&nbsp;
the years, they’ve built up a habit of cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:02.680
corners and turning a blind eye to everything&nbsp;
that doesn’t directly earn them money such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:08.480
quality. According to Carvana’s former employees,&nbsp;
“They’re not inspecting vehicles. They’re taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:13.400
cars in with frame damage and not inspecting the&nbsp;
frame damage. They’re selling you guys cars with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:18.680
unreported accidents and cars with accidents that&nbsp;
haven’t shown up on the car facts yet. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:24.000
out of the seven-day-money-back guarantee period,&nbsp;
you’re screwed.” Since these allegations came out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.800
Carvana has gone ahead and put out a bunch of&nbsp;
articles about how much they care about quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.680
but you can take that for what you will. Another&nbsp;
concerning piece of evidence against Carvana is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:39.000
their management of vehicle titles. Carvana’s&nbsp;
lead lawyer, Paul Breaux is facing a whopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:44.560
57 criminal charges in the state of Illinois. This&nbsp;
includes 27 counts of failure to transfer vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.360
titles and 50 counts of improper use of titling&nbsp;
and registration. Illinois would even go as far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:55.440
as banning Carvana from the entire state on 2&nbsp;
separate occasions. And that’s just the tip of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:01.200
the iceberg. Zooming out, Carvana was selling cars&nbsp;
with hit or miss quality, had a bunch of title and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.360
registration issues, their lead lawyer was facing&nbsp;
dozens of criminal charges, they were facing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.080
state wide bans, they were also facing a class&nbsp;
action lawsuit, they were selling stolen cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.880
and they were getting thousands of complaints&nbsp;
through the better business bureau. If all of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:21.120
wasn’t bad enough, Carvana’s cars were usually&nbsp;
sold with super high markups. In Q1 of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:28.000
for example, Carvana boasted a per car profit&nbsp;
margin of an astounding 61%. Why do people agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.440
to pay these ridiculous markups you ask? Well,&nbsp;
they’re usually just uninformed customers who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:39.360
sold the vision of convenience and not having to&nbsp;
deal with car salesmen, when in reality, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.920
probably be in their best interest to haggle with&nbsp;
a car salesman than buy through Carvana. With all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:49.600
of that being said, this is precisely the type&nbsp;
of company that you would want to avoid with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:59.200
a 10 feet pole, yet Carvana would go onto have&nbsp;
the run of their lives thanks to the pandemic.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:07.360
CONTROVERSIAL FALL:
The pandemic was basically the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:12.760
thing that could have ever happened to Carvana.&nbsp;
With everyone stuck at home and often scared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:18.680
go outside, buying a car completely online was a&nbsp;
more desirable option than ever. Combine this with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:24.640
0% interest rates and massive stimulus checks, and&nbsp;
it wasn’t long before people were casually buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:31.280
cars from Carvana as if it were a $50 gadget you&nbsp;
ordered on whim from Amazon. Combine this with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:37.000
chip shortage which made it nearly impossible&nbsp;
to buy a new car in a timely manner, and used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:43.320
car prices would skyrocket. In fact, for quite&nbsp;
a bit of 2022, you could’ve sold your existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:49.440
car for a higher price than you bought it for. As&nbsp;
such, revenue at Carvana would explode from $3.9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:57.440
billion at the end of 2019 to nearly $15 billion&nbsp;
by mid 2022. Similarly, Carvana stock would rocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:03.200
1100% from the pandemic bottom transforming them&nbsp;
from a multi billion dollar company to a several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:10.360
deca billion company. But eventually, the music&nbsp;
would stop playing for Carvana in mid 2022 as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:15.960
Jerome Powell started jacking up rates. Given that&nbsp;
Carvana’s bread and butter was selling used cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:20.600
with massive price hikes to uninformed customers&nbsp;
who couldn’t really afford them, increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:25.520
interest rates really took a toll on Carvana.&nbsp;
Combine this with the chip shortage finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.880
coming to an end and used car prices cooling down&nbsp;
substantially and Carvana would take a big hit on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:37.400
revenue and an even bigger hit to profit which&nbsp;
brings us into Carvana’s single biggest issue:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:43.080
burning money. Despite selling used cars at&nbsp;
far larger markups than virtually anyone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:49.800
in the industry, the reality was that Carvana was&nbsp;
never really profitable. It turns out that buying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:54.000
shipping, and selling cars across the United&nbsp;
States was an extremely expensive. Not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.160
mention, the hundreds of millions they were&nbsp;
spending on advertising per quarter. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:04.520
this trend only got worse during the pandemic. As&nbsp;
their revenue ballooned, so did their losses which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:10.320
peaked at just under $1.6 billion. Investors,&nbsp;
didn’t really mind these losses when revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:15.040
was growing multiple fold and car sales were&nbsp;
through the roof, but when that narrative changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:20.720
so did the sentiment around the stock. If&nbsp;
declining revenue and losses weren’t bad enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:25.120
Carvana’s corner cutting over the past decade&nbsp;
would finally start coming to light almost in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:31.240
unison. And just as all of that was happening&nbsp;
internally at Carvana, the entire stock market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:36.560
would take a nosedive due to recessionary fears,&nbsp;
high inflation, and high interest rates. Strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.640
companies like Facebook and Netflix themselves&nbsp;
would crash 75%. So, it’s not surprising that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:48.160
Carvana, someone with shaky fundamentals was&nbsp;
hit even harder. Though, it’s still crazy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:55.720
think that this led to a full 99% crash. Since&nbsp;
then, it’s not really clear how much of the stink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.720 --> 00:12:01.040
Carvana has really cleaned up. To be honest, it&nbsp;
doesn’t seem like they care to do much more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:06.200
what regulators and states are forcing them to do&nbsp;
in terms of providing a better service. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:11.000
they have got their act together on the financial&nbsp;
side of things just recently posting their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:17.480
real profit in history of $782 million. And&nbsp;
this has been enough for a lot of investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:23.080
to buy back in. But, that doesn’t change that&nbsp;
if you’re shopping on Carvana, you should still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:29.000
be more than a bit careful as this is the type of&nbsp;
company that you’re dealing with. Unlike Carvana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:34.320
a marketplace with a much more wholesome story is&nbsp;
Craigslist. Check out this video to learn more.

